<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Declaration of the Rights of Man</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen was adopted by the French National Constituent Assembly on 26 August 1789. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas, it declared that men are born and remain free and equal in rights, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> lies with the nation, and that law is the expression of the general will. It became a founding text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and of modern human rights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen.jpg" alt="Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_the_Rights_of_Man_and_of_the_Citizen_in_1789.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The third estate proclaimed itself the National Assembly in June, the fall of the Bastille on 14 July protected it from royal troops, and in August it abolished feudal privileges and issued the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Then the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man</b> of 26 August 1789 reached the island.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In France the National Assembly adopted the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b> in August 1789, weeks after the Storming of the Bastille, and in Poland-Lithuania reformers passed the Constitution of 3 May 1791.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians differ over where the programme came from: Butrus Abu-Manneh finds no Western influence in its founding edict, while Stanford Jay Shaw sees the direct influence of the French <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b> of 1789.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany Justus Möser and Friedrich von Gentz criticised the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In France the abolition of feudalism on the night of 4 August 1789 and the <b>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</b> marked the triumph of liberalism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
